Here's a more detailed implementation of the compiler pipeline:

### Step 1: Define the Grammar

Create a grammar file `src/PL.g4` to support the minimal features required for compiling source code. The grammar should include rules for basic constructs such as variables, expressions, and control flow statements.

```antlr
grammar PL;

// Entry point for the parser
program: statement* EOF;

// Define statements
statement: assignment
          | expressionStatement
          | ifStatement
          | whileStatement
          | block;

// Define an assignment statement
assignment: ID '=' expression ';';

// Define an expression statement
expressionStatement: expression ';';

// Define an if statement
ifStatement: 'if' '(' expression ')' block ('else' block)?;

// Define a while statement
whileStatement: 'while' '(' expression ')' block;

// Define a block of statements
block: '{' statement* '}';

// Define expressions
expression: ID
           | INT
           | expression ('+' | '-' | '*' | '/') expression
           | '(' expression ')';

// Tokens
ID: [a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z0-9_]*;
INT: [0-9]+;
WS: [ \t\r\n]+ -> skip;
```

### Step 2: Implement Backend Classes

In `src/backend.kt`, implement classes that represent the compiled runtime backend objects. These classes will encapsulate the logic for executing the compiled code.

```kotlin
// Backend classes for runtime objects
class RuntimeObject {
    // Base class for all runtime objects
}

class Variable(val name: String, var value: Any) : RuntimeObject() {
    // Represents a variable in the runtime
}

class Function(val name: String, val parameters: List<String>, val body: List<RuntimeObject>) : RuntimeObject() {
    // Represents a function in the runtime
}

class Program(val statements: List<RuntimeObject>) {
    // Represents the entire program
    fun execute() {
        // Logic to execute the program
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Implement Visitor Class

Using Semantic Directed Definitions (SDD), implement a visitor class that will traverse the parse tree generated by the grammar and construct the runtime objects.

```kotlin
import org.antlr.v4.runtime.tree.ParseTreeVisitor

class PLVisitor : PLBaseVisitor<RuntimeObject>() {

    override fun visitProgram(ctx: PLParser.ProgramContext): RuntimeObject {
        val statements = ctx.statement().map { visit(it) }
        return Program(statements)
    }

    override fun visitAssignment(ctx: PLParser.AssignmentContext): RuntimeObject {
        val varName = ctx.ID().text
        val expr = visit(ctx.expression())
        return Variable(varName, expr) // Assuming expr evaluates to a value
    }

    override fun visitIfStatement(ctx: PLParser.IfStatementContext): RuntimeObject {
        // Logic to handle if statements
    }

    override fun visitWhileStatement(ctx: PLParser.WhileStatementContext): RuntimeObject {
        // Logic to handle while statements
    }

    override fun visitExpression(ctx: PLParser.ExpressionContext): RuntimeObject {
        // Logic to evaluate expressions
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Compile and Execute

Finally, implement the logic to compile the source code into runtime objects and execute them.

```kotlin
fun compileAndExecute(sourceCode: String) {
    // Step 1: Parse the source code
    val lexer = PLLexer(CharStreams.fromString(sourceCode))
    val tokens = CommonTokenStream(lexer)
    val parser = PLParser(tokens)
    val tree = parser.program()

    // Step 2: Visit the parse tree to create runtime objects
    val visitor = PLVisitor()
    val program = visitor.visit(tree) as Program

    // Step 3: Execute the program
    program.execute()
}
```

### Conclusion

This implementation outlines a basic compiler pipeline that compiles source code to runtime backend objects, enabling program execution. The grammar supports essential programming constructs, while the backend classes and visitor pattern facilitate the creation and execution of runtime objects.

