Consider a vector A[1…n] of ‘n’ numbers. We define an operation Si,j (1 ≤ i ≤ j ≤ n) on A as A[i] + A[i+1] – A[i+2] + A[i+3] – A[i+4] + …. A[j]. Given A, implement an algorithm to determine all pairs of i and j that results in the highest non-zero negative value of Si,j. The corresponding value of Si,j is also to be returned by the algorithm.

Function Prototype: vector<pair<int,int>> subarray(vector<int> A, int *value);

given Sample: 
Input: A = {-4, 6, -7, 2, 2}
Output: (1,2),(1,4), value = -1

GIVE CPP CODE

targets:
Here's a C++ implementation for the problem:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>
#include <climits>
#include <algorithm>

using namespace std;

vector<pair<int,int>> subarray(vector<int> A, int *value) {
    int n = A.size();

    vector<int> B(n); // Transforming the array to further simplify the problem
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        B[i] = (i % 2 ? 1 : -1) * A[i];
    }

    // Start of the Kadane's Algorithm Logic
    vector<int> dp(n);
    vector<int> start_indices(n);
    dp[0] = B[0];
    start_indices[0] = 1;
    int max_val = dp[0];
    int index = 0; 

    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        if (dp[i - 1] >= 0) {
            dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + B[i];
            start_indices[i] = start_indices[i - 1];
        } else {
            dp[i] = B[i];
            start_indices[i] = i + 1;
        }

        if (dp[i] > max_val) {
            max_val = dp[i];
            index = i;
        }
    }
    // End of the Kadane's Algorithm Logic

    int end_index = index + 1;
    int start_index = start_indices[index];

    // Extracting pairs resulting in highest non-zero negative value
    vector<pair<int, int>> result;
    *value = max_val;
    for (int i = start_index; i <= end_index; ++i) {
        result.push_back(make_pair(start_index, i));
    }

    return result;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> A = {-4, 6, -7, 2, 2};
    int value;
    vector<pair<int,int>> result = subarray(A, &value);

    cout << "Output Pairs: ";
    for (auto p : result) {
        cout << "(" << p.first << "," << p.second << "),";
    }
    cout << " value = " << value << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

This solution uses the Kadane's Algorithm to find the contiguous subarray with the highest negative summation. The time complexity of this algorithm is `O(n)` where `n` is the size of the input array.

To render server-side-rendered content dynamically, you can use the `fetch` function to make an HTTP request to retrieve the desired content and then update the innerHTML of the target div.

First, create an API to serve your server-side-rendered content. For this example, let's assume you have an API endpoint at `https://yourserver.com/api/ssr` that returns the render HTML.

Create a JavaScript function to fetch the server-side-rendered content and insert it into the target div:

```javascript
// Function to get SSR content and insert it into the target div
async function loadSsrContent() {
  // Fetch the server-side-rendered content from the API endpoint
  const response = await fetch('https://yourserver.com/api/ssr');

  // If the fetch is successful, update the innerHTML of the target div
  if (response.ok) {
    const ssrContent = await response.text();
    document.getElementById('ssr').innerHTML = ssrContent;
  } else {
    console.error('Error fetching SSR content:', response.status, response.statusText);
  }
}
```

When you want to load the server-side-rendered content, call this function:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Load the SSR content when the page is loaded
  loadSsrContent();
});
```

This example will insert the server-side-rendered content fetched from `https://yourserver.com/api/ssr` into a div with the id `ssr`.

