Here's a JavaScript script that you can use to render server-side code on a div with id #ssr in a static HTML website:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const ssrDiv = document.getElementById('ssr');

    // Replace 'https://api.example.com/data' with your server-side endpoint
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
        .then(response => {
            if (!response.ok) {
                throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
            }
            return response.json();
        })
        .then(data => {
            // Assuming the server returns an object with a 'message' property
            ssrDiv.innerHTML = `<h2>${data.title}</h2><p>${data.message}</p>`;
        })
        .catch(error => {
            console.error('There was a problem with the fetch operation:', error);
            ssrDiv.innerHTML = '<p>Failed to load content. Please try again later.</p>';
        });
});
```

This script uses the Fetch API to make a GET request to your server-side endpoint and then updates the inner HTML of the `ssrDiv` with the data received from the server.

