To render server-side code on a specific `<div>` element within a static HTML website, you can use JavaScript to fetch data from a server and then dynamically insert that data into the desired `<div>`. Below is an example of how you can achieve this using a simple setup.

### HTML Structure

Create a basic HTML file with a `<div>` where you want to render the server-side content.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Server-Side Rendering Example</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
</head>
<body>
    <div id="content">
        <!-- Server-side content will be rendered here -->
    </div>

    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

### CSS (styles.css)

You can add some basic styles to your CSS file to make it look better.

```css
body {
    font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
    margin: 20px;
}

#content {
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    padding: 20px;
    background-color: #f9f9f9;
}
```

### JavaScript (script.js)

In your JavaScript file, you can use the Fetch API to retrieve server-side data and render it inside the specified `<div>`.

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const contentDiv = document.getElementById('content');

    // Replace 'https://api.example.com/data' with your server-side endpoint
    fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
        .then(response => {
            if (!response.ok) {
                throw new Error('Network response was not ok');
            }
            return response.json();
        })
        .then(data => {
            // Assuming the server returns an object with a 'message' property
            contentDiv.innerHTML = `<h2>${data.title}</h2><p>${data.message}</p>`;
        })
        .catch(error => {
            console.error('There was a problem with the fetch operation:', error);
            contentDiv.innerHTML = '<p>Failed to load content. Please try again later.</p>';
        });
});
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML Structure**: The HTML file contains a `<div>` with the ID `content`, which is where the server-side data will be rendered.

2. **CSS**: Basic styles are applied to the body and the content div to enhance the visual presentation.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - The script waits for the DOM to fully load before executing.
   - It uses the Fetch API to make a GET request to a specified server-side endpoint (replace `'https://api.example.com/data'` with your actual endpoint).
   - Upon receiving a successful response, it parses the JSON data and updates the inner HTML of the `contentDiv` with the data received from the server.
   - If there is an error during the fetch operation, it logs the error and displays a fallback message in the `contentDiv`.

### Note

- Ensure that your server-side endpoint is set up to handle CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) if your static HTML is served from a different origin than your API.
- This example assumes that the server returns a JSON object with properties `title` and `message`. Adjust the rendering logic based on the actual structure of the data returned by your server.

