Creating a simple HTML website that resembles a database can be done using basic HTML, CSS, and a bit of JavaScript for interactivity. Below is an example of a simple HTML structure that displays a table, which can be thought of as a database table. This example includes a form to add new entries to the table.

### Example HTML Code

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Simple Database Example</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            margin: 20px;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
        }
        h1 {
            text-align: center;
        }
        table {
            width: 100%;
            border-collapse: collapse;
            margin-top: 20px;
        }
        th, td {
            border: 1px solid #ddd;
            padding: 8px;
            text-align: left;
        }
        th {
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
        }
        tr:hover {
            background-color: #f1f1f1;
        }
        form {
            margin-top: 20px;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
            max-width: 400px;
            margin: auto;
        }
        input[type="text"], input[type="number"] {
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 5px 0;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 4px;
        }
        input[type="submit"] {
            background-color: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 10px;
            cursor: pointer;
        }
        input[type="submit"]:hover {
            background-color: #45a049;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <h1>Simple Database Example</h1>

    <form id="dataForm">
        <input type="text" id="name" placeholder="Name" required>
        <input type="number" id="age" placeholder="Age" required>
        <input type="text" id="email" placeholder="Email" required>
        <input type="submit" value="Add Entry">
    </form>

    <table id="dataTable">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Name</th>
                <th>Age</th>
                <th>Email</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <!-- Data will be inserted here -->
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <script>
        document.getElementById('dataForm').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            const name = document.getElementById('name').value;
            const age = document.getElementById('age').value;
            const email = document.getElementById('email').value;

            const table = document.getElementById('dataTable').getElementsByTagName('tbody')[0];
            const newRow = table.insertRow();

            const nameCell = newRow.insertCell(0);
            const ageCell = newRow.insertCell(1);
            const emailCell = newRow.insertCell(2);

            nameCell.textContent = name;
            ageCell.textContent = age;
            emailCell.textContent = email;

            // Clear the form
            document.getElementById('dataForm').reset();
        });
    </script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML Structure**: The HTML consists of a form for inputting data (name, age, email) and a table to display the entries.

2. **CSS Styles**: Basic styles are applied to make the table and form look presentable.

3. **JavaScript Functionality**: When the form is submitted, the JavaScript captures the input values, creates a new row in the table, and populates it with the entered data. The form is then reset for new entries.

### How to Use

1. Copy the code above into a text editor and save it as `index.html`.
2. Open the `index.html` file in a web browser.
3. You can enter data into the form and click "Add Entry" to see it appear in the table below.

This is a very basic example and does not include any backend functionality or persistent storage. For a real database application, you would typically use a server-side language (like PHP, Python, Node.js) and a database (like MySQL, MongoDB) to store and retrieve data.

